course: french
lesson: 24
title: Likes and dislikes: j'aime, je n'aime pas
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: French has a ladder for liking things: j'adore (I love), j'aime (I like), j'aime bien (I quite like), je n'aime pas (I don't like) and je déteste (I hate).
explain: To say "not", French wraps two little words around the verb like the bread of a sandwich: je ne mange pas, and ne loses its e before a vowel, so je n'aime pas.
explain: Picture lunch at a French school: someone points at the spinach and shouts "Beurk !" (yuck), someone else grins at the chips and says "Miam !" (yum), and a third shrugs, "Bof...", which means meh, not bothered.
explain: A reason makes your French sound grown-up: "Je déteste les brocolis parce que c'est dégoûtant", I hate broccoli because it's disgusting.
explain: In the street you will hear "J'aime pas" without ne, and teenagers say "Je kiffe" for "I really like" - real French, but in your written work keep "je n'aime pas" and "j'adore".
fact: Ne becomes n' before a vowel: je n'aime pas, je n'ai pas; ne and pas go round the verb.
fact: The scale of liking: j'adore (love), j'aime (like), j'aime bien (quite like), je n'aime pas (don't like), je déteste (hate).
fact: Pourquoi ? means "Why?" and parce que means "because": parce que c'est délicieux, nul, ennuyeux (boring) or drôle (funny).
fact: C'est nul means "It's rubbish"; nul is not a rude word. Its opposite is c'est génial, "It's brilliant".
fact: Je préfère means "I prefer"; after je the second é becomes è: je préfère.
fact: Mais (but) joins a like and a dislike; et (and) joins two likes: J'aime le chocolat mais je n'aime pas le café.
fact: Je kiffe is slang for "I really like"; teenagers use it with friends, not in written work.
fact: In everyday spoken French the ne is often dropped (j'aime pas); in writing and polite speech you keep it (je n'aime pas).
fact: Beurk ! means "Yuck!" and Miam ! or Miam-miam ! means "Yum!"
fact: Bof is a shrugging sound meaning "meh, so-so"; it means not great and not terrible, not "bad".
fact: Qu'est-ce que tu aimes ? means "What do you like?"; qu'est-ce que sounds like "kess-kuh".
exercise: Charlotte writes "Je ne aime pas les chiens." What is right? | Je n'aime pas les chiens | Je n'aime les chiens pas | Je aime pas les chiens | Je ne pas aime les chiens
exercise: Which word on the liking ladder is the strongest? | J'adore | J'aime bien | J'aime | Je préfère
exercise: Which means "I quite like"? | J'aime bien | J'adore | Je déteste | Je n'aime pas
exercise: Your teacher asks "Pourquoi ?" What is she asking? | Why? | When? | Where? | Who?
exercise: How do you say "because it's boring"? | parce que c'est ennuyeux | parce que c'est génial | pourquoi c'est ennuyeux | parce que c'est drôle
exercise: Lewis's friend says "C'est nul !" about the rain. What does she mean? | It's rubbish | It's zero degrees | It's brilliant | It's a rude word
exercise: What is the opposite of c'est nul? | C'est génial | C'est bof | C'est beurk | C'est ennuyeux
exercise: How do you spell "I prefer"? | je préfère | je préfére | je prefere | je préfèr
exercise: Lily likes chocolate but not coffee. Which word joins the two halves? | mais | et | parce que | pourquoi
exercise: Harry writes "Je kiffe le foot" in his test. What's the problem? | It's slang, fine with friends but not for written work | It's a rude word that should never be said | It's not French at all, it's English | It means he hates football
exercise: Aisha's penpal writes "J'aime pas les épinards." Is that a mistake? | No, it's how people speak; in writing keep ne | Yes, it's always wrong to drop ne | Yes, it means she loves spinach | No, ne is never used in French
exercise: Jack's friend shouts "Beurk !" at a spider. What does it mean? | Yuck! | Help! | Wow! | Burp!
exercise: What do you say about yummy food? | Miam ! | Beurk ! | Bof ! | Nul !
exercise: Freya calls the film "bof". What did she think? | It was so-so | It was terrible | It was brilliant | It was scary
exercise: How do you ask "What do you like?" | Qu'est-ce que tu aimes ? | Pourquoi tu aimes ? | Où est-ce que tu aimes ? | Tu aimes qu'est-ce ?
exercise: Josh says "J'adore les frites !" How much does he like chips? | He loves them | He quite likes them | He hates them | He doesn't mind them
test: Your mate goes, "Je ne aime pas sounds fine, bruv, don't be extra." What's the rule? | Before a vowel ne becomes n': je n'aime pas | Je ne aime pas is the polite form | You drop pas before a vowel: je n'aime | It should be je aime ne pas
test: Ava from Ohio says broccoli is "mid" and her teacher asks "Pourquoi ?" (mid = average, nothing special) Which answer is the closest French? | Parce que c'est bof | Parce que c'est génial | Parce que c'est délicieux | Parce que c'est trop bien
test: Gran in Glasgow says, "Nul? That's a swear, hen, wash yer mouth oot!" Is she right? | No, nul just means rubbish and is fine in class | Yes, nul is a very rude French word | Yes, but only when you say it to a teacher | No, nul is a kind word meaning lovely
test: ★ Your sister thinks bof means "really bad". What would she get wrong if a friend said the pizza was bof? | She'd think it was awful when it was just so-so | She'd think it was the best pizza ever | She'd think it was a type of cheese | Nothing, bof does mean really bad
test: A cartoon cat says "Je déteste les souris !" Where is it on the ladder? | At the bottom: it hates mice | At the top: it loves mice | In the middle: it quite likes mice | It prefers mice to fish
test: ★ Why do French teachers want "je n'aime pas" in tests when kids in the street say "j'aime pas"? | Writing keeps ne; talk often drops it | J'aime pas is a different, rude meaning | Street French is not real French | Teachers are stricter than the dictionary
test: ★ Tom says, "J'aime le foot et je n'aime pas le rugby." His teacher says one little word would fit better. Which? | mais | et | parce que | aussi
test: ★★★ Put these in order from most to least liked: j'aime bien, je déteste, j'adore, je n'aime pas, j'aime. | j'adore, j'aime, j'aime bien, je n'aime pas, je déteste | j'aime, j'adore, j'aime bien, je déteste, je n'aime pas | j'adore, j'aime bien, j'aime, je n'aime pas, je déteste | j'aime bien, j'aime, j'adore, je n'aime pas, je déteste
test: ★★★ A French boy says: "Les chiens ? J'adore. Les chats ? Bof. Les araignées ? Beurk !" Which does he like least? | Spiders | Cats | Dogs | Cats and spiders the same
test: Harry from Belfast's penpal says "Je kiffe" and he goes, "Wee bit of slang, eh, I'll stick it in me French exam." Good idea? | No, use j'adore or j'aime in written work | Yes, slang always gets extra marks | Yes, kiffe is the only word for like | No, because kiffe means hate
test: What would happen if you said "Je préfère le basket" to a friend who asked about foot? | They'd know you like basketball more than football | They'd think you hate all sports | They'd think you play basketball for France | They'd think you don't know what foot is
exam: Identify the correct negative form of the statement "J'aime les chiens" in written French. | Je n'aime pas les chiens | Je ne aime pas les chiens | J'aime pas les chiens | Je aime ne pas les chiens
exam: Select the expression denoting the most intense degree of approval upon the conventional scale of preference. | J'adore | J'aime bien | J'aime | Bof
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the interjection "Beurk", as employed in respect of food. | Disgust | Delight | Indifference | Surprise
exam: Determine the conjunction appropriately employed to join a statement of preference to a statement of dislike. | mais | et | pourquoi | donc
